# Exercise: Measure W (Latency)
#
# Little's Law: L = λW
#   L = average number of items in the system
#   λ = throughput (items per unit time)
#   W = average time one item spends in the system
#
# This chapter measures each of L, λ, and W and then verifies the
# law. We start with W — the simplest of the three, because we only
# need to time one item end-to-end.
#
# Setup: a worker strand sleeps 10ms (the "service time") and sends
# the item back. We send one item, time the round trip from send to
# receive, and that elapsed time IS W for this system.
#
# `time.now` returns a microsecond timestamp (not ms — important for
# the arithmetic later in the chapter). The idiomatic pattern for
# elapsed time is to take one timestamp before the work, another
# after, and subtract:
#
#     time.now ...work... time.now swap i.-
#
# Your task: receive the result and compute W (the elapsed time).
# The test asserts the elapsed time is at least 9_000us — anything
# less means the 10ms sleep didn't actually happen.
# When done, delete the marker line below.

# I AM NOT DONE

: test-measure-latency ( -- )
    chan.make   # work channel
    chan.make   # results channel

    # Spawn worker: receive item, sleep 10ms, send to results
    [ over chan.receive drop 10 time.sleep-ms swap chan.send drop drop ] strand.spawn drop

    # Record start time, then send the work item
    time.now                        # ( work results start )
    rot 1 swap chan.send drop       # ( results start )

    # Stack going in: ( results start )
    # Your code here:
    # - receive from the results channel (the channel just below start),
    #   discarding the value itself — we care about timing, not the value
    # - call time.now again and subtract start from it
    # Stack coming out: ( W )    -- W is the elapsed-time-per-item in us
    drop drop 0

    # Do not edit below this line
    # W should be at least 9_000us (sleep was 10ms; allow small jitter)
    9000 i.> test.assert
;
